\section{Problem Formulation and Audited Mechanisms}
\label{sec:method-overview}
We model tool use as a family of constrained partially observable Markov
decision processes indexed by fault group $z\in\mathcal{Z}$.  An episode
produces a verifier-defined binary task outcome and consumes interaction
cost.  \method{} combines four mechanisms: it branches from typed failures in
on-policy trajectories, rewards verifier-observed recovery progress, assigns
a bounded recovery budget across eligible prefixes, and reweights fault
groups toward higher loss while retaining a clean-group floor.

Our empirical target is the incremental behavioral effect of those
mechanisms.  The formal comparison therefore includes GRPO, no-fission, and
no-minimax arms.  The matched arms share the base checkpoint, prompts, task
distribution, primary rollout count, advantage grouping unit, optimizer
schedule, and frozen evaluation episodes.  The two fission-bearing arms also
share the same recovery budget.  No-fission tests
whether the failure-prefix branches help once their estimator consequences
are matched.  No-minimax tests the marginal effect of worst-group
reweighting.  We treat \method{} as an audited composite rather than assuming
that each component is beneficial by construction.

