\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Preregistered primary-suite decision}

Table~\ref{tab:r3-primary} reports verifier-confirmed successes on the frozen
32-episode primary suite.  These values are read from the episode-paired
evaluation artifacts; all comparisons use the same task identities within a
seed.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}
\toprule
Seed & \method{} & GRPO & no-fission & no-minimax & r10 \\
\midrule
23 & 16 & 17 & 16 & 21 & 15 \\
29 & 15 & 14 & 18 & 21 & 15 \\
43 & 17 & 17 & 16 & 20 & 15 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Success counts out of 32 on the frozen primary suite.  The r10
column is the calibrated reference adapter, not a formal mechanism arm.}
\label{tab:r3-primary}
\end{table}

None of the three preregistered hypotheses is supported.  For H1
(\method{} versus GRPO), seed-level paired differences are $-1,+1,0$ and the
pooled difference is 0/96, with a 95\% paired-bootstrap interval of
$[-0.0938,0.0938]$.  For H2 (\method{} versus no-minimax), the differences are
$-5,-6,-3$ and the pooled difference is $-14$/96, with interval
$[-0.2604,-0.0312]$.  For H3 (\method{} versus no-fission), the differences
are $0,-3,+1$ and the pooled difference is $-2$/96, with interval
$[-0.1042,0.0625]$.  Each hypothesis required a common positive direction at
all seeds and a pooled interval excluding zero.  H1--H3 therefore fail.

\subsection{Regression gate and held-out behavior}

The preregistered gate passes: every confidence interval associated with a
nonzero regression warning includes zero.  The null mechanism results are
therefore not caused by one arm collapsing at a single seed.  Relative to the
r10 reference on the held-out suite, pooled paired intervals are
$[-0.0104,0.1562]$ for \method{}, $[0.0104,0.1979]$ for GRPO,
$[0.0312,0.1979]$ for no-fission, and $[0.0521,0.1979]$ for no-minimax.
These secondary results show that formal RL can improve over the calibrated
reference, but they do not support the specific \method{} mechanisms: the two
arms without the claimed components have the clearest held-out gains.

\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{Estimator matching changes the interpretation.}
Failure-prefix fission can appear beneficial when it also creates more
samples or changes the advantage group.  After matching those quantities,
\method{} minus no-fission changes sign across seeds and its pooled interval
contains zero.  In this regime, branching does not induce an identifiable
behavioral improvement beyond the estimator and data-allocation changes that
normally accompany it.

\paragraph{Minimax weighting is the stable negative result.}
No-minimax is better at every seed by 5, 6, and 3 primary-suite episodes.  The
pooled paired interval for \method{} minus no-minimax excludes zero below
zero.  A plausible explanation is that the calibrated policy already sits
near a budget-dependent phase boundary: upweighting weak fault groups spends
limited updates on sparse, high-variance gradients and moves probability
mass away from broadly useful behavior.  This explanation is mechanistic but
not itself causally identified; the empirical conclusion is narrower and
stronger: minimax weighting is harmful under the frozen protocol.

\paragraph{Budget calibration is part of the result.}
Pilot calibration prevented both a near-zero floor and a saturated ceiling.
Even so, 32 binary episodes per seed yield wide intervals for small effects.
The protocol can reliably reject the large positive mechanism story and can
detect the larger minimax penalty, but it cannot rule out modest positive or
negative effects for fission.  Additional rounds chosen after seeing these
results would weaken the preregistration; we therefore stop after the planned
three seeds.

\paragraph{Relation to Fission-GRPO.}
Fission-GRPO is a full recovery-training system with its own simulator,
diagnostic feedback, recovery-instance construction, and benchmark mix
\citep{zhang2026fissiongrpo}.  Our no-fission contrast is not an official
reproduction of that system.  It tests whether the broader branching idea
retains a marginal advantage in our typed executable environments when its
rollout estimator is controlled.  The negative result limits transfer of the
mechanism claim; it does not negate the reported end-to-end Fission-GRPO
results.

\section{Limitations and Broader Impact}
\label{sec:limitations}
The evidence comes from one model family and scale (Qwen3.5-9B with LoRA), a
controlled fault taxonomy, three preregistered seeds, and small frozen binary
evaluation suites.  The paired design improves sensitivity but does not make
small effects identifiable.  Our structured diagnostics and exact state
verifiers are stronger than signals available from many production APIs.

We planned a zero-touch transfer evaluation after freezing all adapters.
ToolBench-X technically fits the tool interface but its dataset terms require
prior permission for copying or modification.  ToolMaze exposes no clearly
applicable code or dataset license.  We did not download or adapt either
dataset and report no external transfer score.  This legal and evidentiary
boundary matters: controlled-environment findings have not been externally
validated.

Tool-recovery training can increase agent autonomy.  Deployment should retain
authorization boundaries, idempotency controls, audit logs, and human
confirmation for irreversible actions.  Negative findings should likewise
not be generalized into a claim that recovery training never works; they show
that component benefits must be demonstrated under matched estimators and
explicitly accounted budgets.

